f_epa_per_play":0.1264},{"week":15,"opponent":"NYJ","home":false,"pf":29.0,"pa":22.0,"off_snaps":50,"epa_per_play":0.04825,"pass_rate":0.68,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6944,"def_epa_per_play":-0.03731},{"week":16,"opponent":"PHI","home":false,"pf":30.0,"pa":32.0,"off_snaps":63,"epa_per_play":0.1394,"pass_rate":0.6984,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6098,"def_epa_per_play":0.1345},{"week":17,"opponent":"JAX","home":true,"pf":20.0,"pa":3.0,"off_snaps":73,"epa_per_play":-0.05519,"pass_rate":0.5616,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5161,"def_epa_per_play":-0.4586}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2025,"team":"CLE","distance":0.5758,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Kevin Stefanski"},{"season":2023,"team":"NE","distance":0.7349,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"PHI","distance":0.8226,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"NE","distance":0.8877,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"CHI","distance":0.8955,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Ben Johnson"}],"defense":[{"season":2022,"team":"TEN","distance":0.9116,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"IND","distance":0.922,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"TEN","distance":1.011,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"NO","distance":1.015,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2022,"team":"CHI","distance":1.016,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Texans went 11-5 in 2018 and outscored opponents 402 to 316. The defense did most of the work, finishing 5th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 18th in EPA per play. What set them apart was the highest 12 personnel usage and the highest Cover 2 rate in the league.","offense":"The Texans offense was about average by EPA per play, and it was much better passing the ball than running it. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the highest 12 personnel usage and the 2nd lowest 11 personnel usage in the league. Deshaun Watson ran the passing game and leaned heavily on DeAndre Hopkins, who drew 33% of the targets.","defense":"The Texans had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 8th or better in all five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was EPA allowed per rush, where it ranked 1st, and its weakest was pressure rate, where it ranked 31st. J.J. Watt led the team with 16 sacks, and Christian Covington added 3.5 despite playing only 33% of the snaps. Schematically, they had the highest Cover 2 rate in the league.","special":"The Texans were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 4% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 88% of field goals, which was a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 20% of the money on the books. Their offensive line was among the youngest in the league.","players":"Deshaun Watson played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,165 yards. DeAndre Hopkins was the clear top target with 33% of the looks and 1,572 receiving yards. Lamar Miller handled most of the work in the backfield with 56% of the carries. On defense, J.J. Watt led the team with 16 sacks and Justin Reid picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2025 Browns under Kevin Stefanski and the 2023 Patriots. Their defense looked most like the 2022 Titans and the 2019 Colts.","trends":"2018 is the first season covered here, so there is no earlier year to compare it with. The charts below show how the Texans changed over the seasons that followed.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2018. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}